# Deploy {{.Environment}}

Do the steps in order. Run commands from the repo root.

Before you start: install `terraform`, `gh` and `gcloud`. Log in to both (`gh auth login`, `gcloud auth login`). Terraform also needs `gcloud auth application-default login`.
{{- if .IsSmall}}

## 1. Fill in the settings

Open `deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform/terraform.tfvars`. Replace `PROJECT_ID`, every `CHANGE_ME` and `OWNER/REPO`.

- `ssh_allowed_cidr`: your IP with `/32` at the end. Get your IP with `curl -4 ifconfig.me`. Only this IP can SSH in.
- `github_runner_token`: get it with the command below. **It stops working after 1 hour.** Get it right before step 2.

```sh
gh api -X POST repos/OWNER/REPO/actions/runners/registration-token --jq .token
```

Git ignores this file. Do not commit it.

## 2. Create the server

```sh
cd deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform
terraform init
terraform apply
```

On first start, the server runs `terraform/runner-init.sh`. It installs Docker and a GitHub runner. Wait a few minutes. Then check the runner is there: repo → Settings → Actions → Runners.

Runner not there? The token probably expired. Get a new token, put it in `terraform.tfvars`, and rebuild the server:

```sh
terraform apply -replace=google_compute_instance.main
```

Editing `runner-init.sh` later does not change the server.

**Do not lose `terraform.tfstate`.** Terraform needs it to change or delete what it made. Git ignores it. Back it up, or move it to a bucket: see the `backend "gcs"` block at the top of `main.tf`.

## 3. Set up the server

SSH in and become root:

```sh
ssh ubuntu@$(terraform output -raw public_ip)
sudo -i
```

The server needs a key to read your repo. Make one and print it:

```sh
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N "" -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
```

Add it on GitHub: repo → Settings → Deploy keys → Add. Leave "write access" off. Then clone:

```sh
git clone git@github.com:OWNER/REPO.git /root/{{.Name}}
```

Create `/root/{{.Name}}/deployments/{{.Environment}}/.env` with this, and replace every `CHANGE_ME`:

```sh
ENVIRONMENT={{.Environment}}
ADDRESS=:4000
{{- if .HasPostgres}}
POSTGRES_USER=app
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
POSTGRES_DB={{.Name}}
GOOSE_DBSTRING=postgres://app:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/{{.Name}}?sslmode=disable
{{- end}}
{{- if .HasRedis}}
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0
{{- end}}
{{- if .IsGrpc}}
DOMAIN=api.example.com
{{- else}}
TUNNEL_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME
{{- end}}
```
{{- if .HasPostgres}}

Use the same password in `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` and `GOOSE_DBSTRING`.
{{- end}}
{{- if .IsGrpc}}

## 4. Domain

Add an A record for your `DOMAIN` that points to the server IP (`terraform output public_ip`). If your DNS is on Cloudflare, turn the proxy off (grey cloud).

On first start, Caddy gets an HTTPS certificate by itself. Clients connect to `DOMAIN:443`.
{{- else}}

## 4. Domain

Your domain's DNS must be on Cloudflare.

1. Cloudflare → Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels → create a tunnel.
2. Copy the tunnel token into `TUNNEL_TOKEN` in `.env`.
3. Add a public hostname, for example `api.example.com`, with service `http://{{.Name}}:4000`.

Cloudflare serves HTTPS for you. No ports are open on the server.
{{- end}}

## 5. Deploy

GitHub → Actions → Deploy {{.Environment}} → Run workflow.

The workflow runs on your server. It pulls the code, builds it, {{if .HasPostgres}}runs the database migration, {{end}}and restarts the app.

**It always deploys the `main` branch**, even if you pick another branch in GitHub.

## Add your own environment variables

Add them to `/root/{{.Name}}/deployments/{{.Environment}}/.env` on the server. Then run the Deploy workflow again.

Put secrets (passwords, API keys) there too. Git ignores `.env`, so they stay on the server.
{{- else}}

## 1. Once per GCP project

Create the GCP project and link a billing account. Terraform turns on the APIs it needs.

## 2. Fill in the settings

Open `deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform/terraform.tfvars`. Replace `PROJECT_ID` and `OWNER/REPO`. Change `region` if you want.

Git ignores this file. Do not commit it. Later it will hold your secrets.

## 3. Create the cloud resources

```sh
cd deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform
terraform init
terraform apply
```

This takes a while. The app runs a placeholder image until your first deploy.

Your app's address:

```sh
terraform output url
```

**Do not lose `terraform.tfstate`.** Terraform needs it to change or delete what it made. It also holds your passwords, so keep it private. Git ignores it. Before anyone else runs Terraform, move it to a bucket: see the `backend "gcs"` block at the top of `main.tf`.

## 4. Connect GitHub

Still in `deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform`. Replace `OWNER/REPO`, then run:

```sh
gh api -X PUT repos/OWNER/REPO/environments/{{.Environment}}
for v in gcp_project_id gcp_region gcp_workload_identity_provider gcp_service_account; do
  gh variable set "$(echo "$v" | tr a-z A-Z)" --env {{.Environment}} --body "$(terraform output -raw "$v")"
done
```

This makes a GitHub environment called `{{.Environment}}` and saves the Terraform outputs in it. They are not secret. GCP only accepts deploys from this environment of this repo.

## 5. Deploy

GitHub → Actions → Deploy {{.Environment}} → Run workflow.

The workflow runs `deployments/{{.Environment}}/deploy.sh`. It builds the image, {{if .HasPostgres}}runs the database migration, {{end}}then starts the new version. If the new version does not start, the old one keeps serving.

To deploy from your computer instead, log in with `gcloud auth login` and install Docker. **Commit your changes first**: the image is named after your last commit. Then:

```sh
cd deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform
export GCP_REGION=$(terraform output -raw gcp_region)
export GCP_PROJECT_ID=$(terraform output -raw gcp_project_id)
cd -
bash deployments/{{.Environment}}/deploy.sh
```

## Add your own environment variables

All changes go in `deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform/main.tf`, inside `resource "google_cloud_run_v2_service" "app"`.

### Normal values

Add an `env` block next to the other `env` blocks in `containers`:

```hcl
      env {
        name  = "LOG_LEVEL"
        value = "info"
      }
```

### Secret values (passwords, API keys)

**Never write a secret value in `main.tf`.** Git tracks that file. Put the value in `terraform.tfvars`. Git ignores that file.

The example adds `JWT_SECRET`. Change `jwt` and `JWT_SECRET` to your own names.

1. Add the value to `terraform.tfvars`:

   ```hcl
   jwt_secret = "the-real-value"
   ```

2. Add this to `main.tf`. The last part lets the app read the secret. **If you skip it, the app will not start.**

   ```hcl
   variable "jwt_secret" {
     type      = string
     sensitive = true
   }

   resource "google_secret_manager_secret" "jwt" {
     secret_id = "${local.name}-jwt"

     replication {
       auto {}
     }

     depends_on = [google_project_service.main]
   }

   resource "google_secret_manager_secret_version" "jwt" {
     secret      = google_secret_manager_secret.jwt.id
     secret_data = var.jwt_secret
   }

   resource "google_secret_manager_secret_iam_member" "jwt" {
     secret_id = google_secret_manager_secret.jwt.id
     role      = "roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor"
     member    = "serviceAccount:${google_service_account.run.email}"
   }
   ```

3. Add an `env` block next to the other `env` blocks in `containers`:

   ```hcl
         env {
           name = "JWT_SECRET"
           value_source {
             secret_key_ref {
               secret  = google_secret_manager_secret.jwt.secret_id
               version = "latest"
             }
           }
         }
   ```

4. Make the app wait for the secret. At the end of the service resource,
{{- if .HasPostgres}} add the two `jwt` items to the `depends_on` list:

   ```hcl
     depends_on = [
       google_secret_manager_secret_version.database_url,
       google_secret_manager_secret_iam_member.database_url,
       google_secret_manager_secret_version.jwt,
       google_secret_manager_secret_iam_member.jwt,
     ]
   ```
{{- else}} add:

   ```hcl
     depends_on = [
       google_secret_manager_secret_version.jwt,
       google_secret_manager_secret_iam_member.jwt,
     ]
   ```
{{- end}}

### Make the change live

```sh
cd deployments/{{.Environment}}/terraform
terraform apply
```

Then run the Deploy workflow again. **A changed secret value does not reach the running app until the next deploy.**

## If you delete this environment

After `terraform destroy`, wait about 30 days before you create `{{.Environment}}` again in the same project. GCP keeps its GitHub login pool (`github-{{.Environment}}`) that long and will not reuse the name.
{{- end}}
{{- if .HasPostgres}}

## Database migrations

Each deploy runs `build/migrate.sh` (`goose up`) before it starts the new app. If a migration fails, it is undone, and the old app keeps running.

Rules for every migration:

- Write a working `-- +goose Down`.
- Only add things. Do not drop or rename columns the running app still uses.
- Be careful with `-- +goose NO TRANSACTION` (for example `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`). It can fail halfway. A failed concurrent index stays behind as an invalid index, and you must drop it by hand.
{{- end}}
